 6. ⚠️ Climate catastrophe risk rising 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Houses lie deluged in sludge after massive floods in Nepal this week. More than 390 people have been killed along the Nepal-China border, with more than 1,400 missing. Photo: Prabin Ranabhat/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Scientists have long warned that the world will experience more intense extreme weather events, including heat waves, storms, floods and droughts. 

 But scenarios that once seemed like the realm of fiction are now discussed as a possibility, even an inevitability, AP writes. "The risk is that people just think you are exaggerating," said Daniel Swain, a climate scientist with the California Institute for Water Resources. "It's the 'Boy Who Cried Wolf' problem. Sometimes wolves are real. If it's outside the door, wouldn't you want to know?"

