 8. 🐊 1 swamp thing: Return of gator hunting 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Alligator hunting in bayou waters in Terrebonne Parish near Houma, La., in 2019. Photo: Drew Angerer/Getty Images Louisiana is reopening gator hunting for the first time in more than 60 years following a resurgence from endangered status, Axios New Orleans' Chelsea Brasted writes. 

 The big picture: The state's gators had been hunted since the 1800s, eventually resulting in a severely depleted population.

 Louisiana banned gator hunting in 1962 to allow the species a chance to naturally rebound. It worked. And a strategy of targeted farming and limited hunting helped alligators make a "full recovery," according to the Louisiana Department of Wildlife and Fisheries. Subscribe to Axios Local . 

 
 
